  grid: 10-19 rows x 10-19 cols, background 0 (black)
  lattice:
    color: 2 (red) [observed]
    pitch: 5 in both dimensions (odd, >=3 required by solver)
    marker_rows: 3-4 evenly spaced rows of single-pixel markers
    marker_cols: 2-4 evenly spaced columns of single-pixel markers
    first_marker: row 0-2, col 0-2
    grid_sized_so: each non-boundary marker has pitch rows and cols of space below-right
  cells:
    definition: a marker (r,c) defines a cell if r+pitch <= H and c+pitch <= W
    count: 6 [observed]; cell_rows x cell_cols in {2x3, 3x2} [observed]
    boundary_markers: last marker row/col may lack room for cells; they serve as visual boundary
  sprites:
    color: 1 (blue) [observed]
    count: one per cell (= 6 [observed])
    bbox: always 2x2
    pixels: 3 (L-triomino, 4 orientations) or 4 (full square); 4-6 triominoes + 0-2 squares per example
    input_offset: top-left of bbox at (1-3, 1-3) relative to cell marker; not all at center
    output_offset: all moved to center (pitch//2, pitch//2) = (2,2), shape preserved
  transform: clear each sprite from input position, repaint at cell center; markers unchanged

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick cell_rows x cell_cols from {2x3, 3x2}; set pitch=5
    2. Pick first_marker position (row 0-2, col 0-2)
    3. Compute marker grid; add 0-1 extra boundary marker rows and cols beyond last cell marker
    4. Set grid size = last_marker + 1 + random(0-4) padding per axis, ensuring cell validity
    5. For each cell, pick a random 2x2-bbox sprite (3 or 4 pixels); place at center (2,2) -> this is the output
    6. To build input, copy output and for each sprite, move it from center to a random non-center offset (1-3, 1-3) within the cell; ensure >=4 sprites are off-center

---
spec:
  grid: 16-26 rows x 14-24 cols, bg=0
  outlines:
    count: 1-4, each a distinct color, 4-connected monochromatic
    closed: 1-3 per grid; 1-pixel-thick rectilinear loops with 8-14 corners
    open: 0-1 per grid; 1-pixel-thick path with 2 endpoints, no enclosed region [observed]
    area: 10-46 pixels; bbox 4x5 to 13x11
    non-overlapping: outlines do not share cells or overlap bboxes, min manhattan distance >= 3
  markers:
    single isolated pixels (area=1), scattered on bg
    total: 7-32 across the grid
    per enclosed region: 3-7 markers with 2-3 distinct colors; one color is strictly most frequent (majority)
    stray markers: 0-14 pixels outside all enclosed regions, removed in output
  colors: outline colors and marker colors may overlap (test_0) but each outline has a unique color
  rule:
    keep all outline pixels unchanged
    fill each enclosed interior with the majority marker color found inside it
    remove all isolated marker pixels (both stray and inside-region ones replaced by fill)
    open outlines and bg unchanged

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose grid size in [16-26] x [14-24].
    2. Choose 1-4 outline colors (distinct). For each, decide closed (loop) or open (path), with at least 1 closed and at most 1 open.
    3. Build closed outlines as 1-pixel-thick rectilinear loops (irregular polygons with 8-14 corners, bbox 6x6 to 13x11). Place non-overlapping on the grid with min manhattan gap >= 3.
    4. Optionally build 1 open outline as a 1-pixel-thick rectilinear path (no enclosed region).
    5. For each closed outline, flood-fill its enclosed interior to determine the fill region.
    6. For each enclosed region, pick a majority marker color (distinct from outline colors and bg). Place 2-6 marker pixels of that color inside the region, plus 1-2 minority-color markers (fewer count than majority). Record the fill color = majority color.
    7. The output is: outlines + filled interiors. The input is: outlines + marker pixels inside regions (unfilled interior is bg) + 0-14 stray marker pixels scattered on bg cells outside all outlines.

---
spec:
  grid: 12-15 rows x 13-16 cols
  background: 0
  foreground: single color from {1,3,5,6,7,8,9} (not 0, 2, or 4)
  cycles:
    count: 2-3 non-overlapping, non-nested, simply-connected rectilinear closed loops
    structure: 1-pixel-wide 4-connected; every pixel has exactly degree 2
    perimeter_pixels: 8-42 per cycle
    convex_corners: 4-12 (majority turn direction)
    concave_corners: 0-8 (minority turn direction)
    invariant: convex - concave = 4 always
    min_gap_between_cycles: >=2 (Manhattan distance)
    bbox: 3x3 to 10x12 per cycle
    interior: single connected region of background pixels [observed]
  output_transform: |
    Straight pixels (no turn) keep foreground color.
    Convex bend pixels (majority turn sign per cycle) become yellow (4).
    Concave bend pixels (minority turn sign per cycle) become red (2).

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction of rectilinear cycles
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size and foreground color per spec.
    2. For each cycle (2-3 total), generate a simply-connected rectilinear polygon
       boundary by starting with a rectangle and adding/removing rectangular notches
       to create concave corners. Ensure every boundary pixel has exactly degree 2.
    3. Place cycles on grid with no overlap and Manhattan gap >=2 between any pair.
       Bounding boxes must not nest.
    4. Verify each cycle has convex - concave = 4 (guaranteed by simple-connectivity).
    5. Fill grid background with 0, cycle pixels with foreground color.

---
spec:
  grid: 13-15 rows x 13-15 cols, bg=0
  colors: bg=0, skeleton=4, anchor=2, markers={1,3} [observed — solver infers roles dynamically]
  motifs:
    count: 3-4 total (1 reference + 2-3 incomplete targets)
    skeleton: 4-connected polyomino, 5-6 cells, fits in 3-5 x 2-5 bbox
    anchor: exactly 1 pixel per motif, color shared across all, appears once per motif
    markers: 3-5 extra-color pixels on bg cells 8-adjacent to skeleton (reference only)
    marker_colors: 2 distinct colors [observed]
    structure: all motifs are distinct dihedral (rot90/flip) variants of the same skeleton+anchor shape
    uniqueness: skeleton+anchor combo must have >=N distinct dihedral variants where N = motif count
    asymmetry: anchor breaks skeleton symmetry so each target has exactly 1 valid alignment
  layout:
    motifs separated by >=4 Manhattan distance
    stamped markers land on bg cells within grid bounds, no overlap with other motifs
  transform:
    find unique dihedral alignment mapping reference skeleton+anchor onto each target
    stamp transformed marker pixels onto target (on bg cells), skeleton unchanged

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Design a 4-connected skeleton polyomino (5-6 cells) and place anchor on a bg cell 8-adjacent to it
    2. Verify skeleton+anchor has enough dihedral variants (>=N for N=3-4 motifs)
    3. Place 3-5 marker pixels (2 colors) on bg cells 8-adjacent to skeleton to form the reference patch
    4. Pick N-1 distinct dihedral variants for target motifs; for each, compute transformed marker positions
    5. Choose grid size 13-15 x 13-15; place all motifs (reference + targets) with >=4 Manhattan separation
    6. Verify all transformed marker positions land on bg cells within grid bounds (this is the output)
    7. Remove marker pixels from target motifs to produce the input (targets keep only skeleton+anchor)

